the book was really Britain's as a  letter to a younger version of myself  who didn't realize that the journey  itself is more important than this  destination I was really caught up for  my very first year of graduate school  and we all used to think about what  could we do to win a Nobel prizes the  ultimate a plus  but along the way when you focus on  these awards say an actor just purely  focused on winning an Oscar or a  director that causes a person to have  tunnel vision to focus intently on just  achieving a single goal and I think that  goal orientation reflects and can do  damage to to a scientist integrity into  the way that they conduct themselves I  [Music]  think it actually in many cases is  punishing scientists and setting up  artificial collaborations that  disintegrate because the competition  that exists to win the Nobel Prize to  compete for the dwindling amount of  resources that are available and also  that the the prize is really not  representative of the spectrum that is  modern science its diverse its men and  women it's from all countries it's not  just three people at most sitting in a  room I think it's it's demeaning to  science to say that this is the ultimate  thing to aspire for whereas the  scientific journey is reward and should  be rewarded in itself  [Music]  and I think we have to act now  I think it's an urgent time we can  actually transform it before it's too  late  change is difficult and I think my book  is written in such a way to give  concrete ideas for people to transform  this accolade so that it is this guild  of lustrous thing that is worthy of  aspiration and in doing so reflect how  science is done today  [Music]
 

